  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Two girls fooled the creator of Sherlock Holmes. Cottingley, Yorkshire, nineteen seventeen, with a borrowed camera.",
        "visual_query": "yorkshire garden stream trees",
        "visual_fallback": "green garden"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Elsie was sixteen and worked at a photographer's. Frances was nine and had told her mother she played with fairies by the beck.",
        "visual_query": "vintage box camera closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "old camera"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Arthur Conan Doyle got hold of the prints. He was a spiritualist, he was convinced, and he published them in The Strand.",
        "visual_query": "1920s magazine printing press",
        "visual_fallback": "old magazine"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Experts of the day argued about the negatives for years. Nobody thought to ask a sixteen-year-old how she did it.",
        "visual_query": "photographic negatives darkroom light",
        "visual_fallback": "darkroom prints"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In nineteen eighty-three both women admitted it: paper cutouts propped on hatpins. Frances insisted to the end that one photo was real.",
        "visual_query": "paper cutout figures grass",
        "visual_fallback": "paper cutouts"
      }
    ],
